-- Original lesson summaries adapted from the school Medicine and Elizabeth guides
-- in SchoolRevisionGuides/History and checked against Edexcel History 1HI0 Issue 6.
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO topic_lessons
  (topic_id, summary, learning_objectives_json, key_points_json, exam_tips_json, worked_example_json, practice_questions_json)
VALUES
  ('history-edexcel-medieval',
   'Medieval explanations combined religious and supernatural ideas with the Four Humours and miasma. Treatments followed those beliefs, while physicians, apothecaries, barber surgeons and families provided different kinds of care.',
   '["Compare medieval explanations of illness","Connect prevention and treatment to beliefs about disease","Explain responses to the Black Death"]',
   '["Hippocrates and Galen influenced the Four Humours and balancing treatments","The Church shaped medical learning and care","Bloodletting, purging, prayer and herbal remedies reflected different explanations","The Black Death is the Edexcel case study for this period"]',
   '["Name the belief before explaining the treatment it led to","Use the Black Death as a specific example","Distinguish physician, apothecary and barber surgeon roles"]',
   '{"title":"Link belief to treatment","prompt":"Why was bloodletting used?","steps":["State the Four Humours theory","Explain that imbalance was believed to cause illness","Show how removing blood was intended to restore balance"],"answer":"Doctors who accepted the Four Humours thought illness could result from an imbalance, so bloodletting aimed to restore that balance."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one way medieval beliefs shaped treatment of illness.","hint":"Choose one belief and one matching treatment.","answer":"For example, the Four Humours theory led to bloodletting because removing blood was thought to restore balance.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-renaissance',
   'Renaissance medicine tested older ideas more openly, but many traditional approaches continued. Vesalius studied anatomy, Harvey explained blood circulation, Sydenham improved diagnosis, and the Great Plague exposed limits in prevention.',
   '["Explain change and continuity in medical ideas","Describe the significance of Vesalius, Harvey and Sydenham","Compare responses to the Great Plague with medieval responses"]',
   '["Printing and the Royal Society helped spread and test ideas","Vesalius challenged errors in Galen through human dissection","Harvey described the circulation of blood","Sydenham observed symptoms to identify diseases","The Great Plague of 1665 is a named Edexcel case study"]',
   '["Explain why a discovery mattered as well as what was discovered","Avoid claiming that new anatomy immediately changed all treatments"]',
   '{"title":"Explain significance","prompt":"Why was Harvey’s work important?","steps":["Identify the discovery of blood circulation","Explain how it challenged Galen","Judge its immediate limits for treatment"],"answer":"Harvey’s evidence challenged Galen’s account of blood flow and improved understanding of the body, although treatments did not change at once."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one reason medical understanding changed during the Renaissance.","hint":"Use a named person or development and explain its effect.","answer":"A valid answer might explain how Vesalius used human dissection to identify errors in Galen’s anatomy.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-industrial',
   'Between c1700 and c1900, germ theory and experiments changed explanations of disease. Vaccination, anaesthetics, antiseptics and public-health action changed prevention and treatment, although progress was uneven.',
   '["Explain why germ theory was a turning point","Compare prevention, surgery and public health","Use specific individuals and case studies as evidence"]',
   '["Jenner developed smallpox vaccination before germ theory","Pasteur’s germ theory and Koch’s work linked microbes with disease","Anaesthetics reduced pain; antiseptic and aseptic methods addressed infection","Public-health reform responded to urban disease","Cholera and John Snow form a named Edexcel case study"]',
   '["Keep the dates of Jenner, Pasteur, Koch and Snow in sequence","Explain why anaesthetics alone did not solve surgical infection"]',
   '{"title":"Compare two changes","prompt":"Why did surgery improve in the nineteenth century?","steps":["Identify the problem of pain","Explain the effect of anaesthetics","Identify infection as a separate problem","Explain antiseptic or aseptic methods"],"answer":"Anaesthetics allowed longer operations by reducing pain, while antiseptic and later aseptic methods reduced infection, which anaesthetics alone could not prevent."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one way understanding of disease changed in the nineteenth century.","hint":"Use germ theory and a named piece of evidence.","answer":"Pasteur’s germ theory challenged explanations such as miasma by showing that microbes could cause disease.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-modern-medicine',
   'Modern medicine has changed through genetics, diagnostic technology, new drugs and wider access to care. The school guide organises this period around causes, prevention, treatment and care.',
   '["Explain changing ideas about causes of disease","Describe twentieth-century prevention and treatment","Assess the role of the NHS and technology"]',
   '["Genetic and lifestyle factors broadened explanations of illness","Blood tests, scans and monitors improved diagnosis","Antibiotics and other drugs changed treatment","The NHS widened access to healthcare","Government campaigns and vaccination contributed to prevention"]',
   '["Separate changes in understanding from changes in access to care","Use a named example when judging the role of government or technology"]',
   '{"title":"Judge a factor","prompt":"How did government influence modern medicine?","steps":["Name a government action","Explain its effect on access or prevention","Compare it with the role of science"],"answer":"The NHS widened access to treatment, while scientific advances such as antibiotics changed what treatment could achieve; both factors worked together."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one way the NHS changed medical care in Britain.","hint":"Focus on access to care.","answer":"A developed answer explains that NHS provision widened access to medical care, allowing more people to receive treatment regardless of their ability to pay directly.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-elizabeth-government',
   'Elizabeth inherited religious division, financial weakness and foreign threats in 1558. Her government and religious settlement sought stability while facing both Catholic and Puritan criticism.',
   '["Explain Elizabeth’s position in 1558","Describe how government worked","Explain the religious settlement and early challenges"]',
   '["Legitimacy, marriage and succession affected Elizabeth’s authority","The monarch, Privy Council and Parliament had distinct roles","The 1559 religious settlement aimed for a workable Church of England","Catholic and Puritan objections challenged the settlement"]',
   '["Tie each challenge to a specific date or group","Explain how Elizabeth responded instead of listing problems"]',
   '{"title":"Explain a decision","prompt":"Why was a religious settlement important in 1559?","steps":["Identify religious division","State what Elizabeth sought to secure","Explain how a settlement could support stability"],"answer":"Elizabeth faced a divided country, so a settlement defining worship and royal authority aimed to reduce conflict and strengthen her rule."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one problem Elizabeth faced on becoming queen.","hint":"Choose legitimacy, religion, marriage, finance or a foreign threat.","answer":"For example, religious division threatened stability because Catholics and Protestants disagreed about the form of the English Church.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-elizabeth-challenges',
   'Plots involving Mary Queen of Scots, tensions with Spain and war in the Netherlands challenged Elizabeth. The Spanish Armada of 1588 was the most dramatic foreign threat.',
   '["Explain why Mary Queen of Scots was a threat","Trace the growth of conflict with Spain","Explain the causes and outcome of the Armada"]',
   '["The Northern Earls’ Revolt and Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots raised fears of Catholic rebellion","Walsingham’s spy network helped uncover plots","Religious, political and commercial rivalry increased tension with Spain","Events in the Netherlands and Drake’s raids worsened relations","The Armada failed in 1588"]',
   '["Keep plots in chronological order","Distinguish causes of the Armada from reasons for its failure","Use specific evidence for a judgement about the greatest threat"]',
   '{"title":"Build a causal chain","prompt":"Why did relations with Spain worsen?","steps":["State an existing source of rivalry","Add a specific action in the Netherlands or at sea","Explain why it increased the chance of war"],"answer":"Religious and commercial rivalry already strained relations; English support for Dutch rebels and attacks on Spanish interests then increased Philip II’s hostility."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one reason Mary Queen of Scots threatened Elizabeth.","hint":"Connect her claim or religion to a named plot.","answer":"Mary was a Catholic with a claim to the throne, making her a focus for plots that sought to remove Elizabeth.","marks":4}]'),
  ('history-edexcel-elizabeth-society',
   'Elizabethan society changed through education, leisure and exploration, while poverty remained a serious problem. Voyages by Drake and Raleigh show both opportunities and limits of overseas expansion.',
   '["Describe education and leisure in Elizabethan society","Explain responses to poverty","Explain motives and outcomes of exploration"]',
   '["Education and leisure depended strongly on social position and wealth","Rising poverty led to changing attitudes and policies","Theatre and other pastimes expanded","Navigation and trade encouraged exploration","Raleigh’s Virginia attempts did not establish a lasting colony under Elizabeth"]',
   '["Avoid treating all Elizabethans as equally wealthy or educated","Separate motives for exploration from its outcomes"]',
   '{"title":"Explain a consequence","prompt":"Why did exploration matter to Elizabethan England?","steps":["Identify a motive such as trade","Name a voyage or figure","Explain an outcome and its limit"],"answer":"Exploration opened new trading ambitions, but Raleigh’s Virginia ventures show that early attempts at colonisation could fail."}',
   '[{"question":"Explain one reason poverty was a concern in Elizabethan England.","hint":"Link a cause to its social effect.","answer":"A developed answer names a cause of increasing poverty and explains why it made relief or control a concern for government.","marks":4}]');
